ctured, or salvage) is not indicated. |\n| 3 — “Timing belt tensioner, qty 2” | Check | Part tier is not indicated. |\n| 4 — “Labor — replace timing belt, 4.0 hr @ 140.00/hr” | Standard | Operation, hours, and rate are shown. |\n| 5 — “Labor — replace timing belt, 4.0 hr @ 140.00/hr” | Question this | Same operation, hours, and rate as line 4; ask whether this is a duplicate charge or a distinct operation. |\n| 6 — “Labor — remove and reinstall engine cover, 0.5 hr @ 140.00/hr” | Standard | Operation, hours, and rate are shown. |\n| 7 — “Access labor” | Check | Labor basis and rate are not shown. |\n| 8 — “Serpentine belt (Gates)” | Check | The part’s OEM/aftermarket/remanufactured/salvage tier is not identified. |\n| 9 — “Sales tax 7%” | Standard | A tax rate is stated. |\n| — — “TOTAL” | Standard | The listed total is consistent with the listed line amounts and stated tax rate. |\n\n## Questions to ask\n1. “The estimate lists timing-belt replacement labor twice, at 4.0 hours each. Is the second entry a duplicate, or does it cover a distinct operation?” — A good answer identifies exactly what each labor line covers and clarifies whether both are intended.\n2. “What does the ‘Access labor’ line cover, and how is its labor time or rate determined?” — A good answer describes the work and gives its labor basis, including whether it overlaps with another labor line.\n3. “What are the part tiers and part numbers for the idler pulley kit, the two tensioners, and the serpentine belt?” — A good answer identifies the parts and whether each is OEM, aftermarket, remanufactured, or salvage.\n4. “Is the serpentine belt included as part of this timing-belt service, or is it a separate recommended item?” — A good answer explains why it is on this estimate and whether it was authorized.\n\n## Decision\nThe checklist is yours; you decide what to do. Photograph the estimate, note what was promised verbally and by whom, and keep the paperwork chain.\n\n## Needs confirmation\n[NOT SHOW